                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Icycle is another brilliant example of an indie studio creating an absolute masterpiece. I love discovering indie gems, and this is definitely one of my favourites. I remember playing it on my iPhone back in around 2018, so one night, out of pure curiosity, I decided to Google it and see if it still existed. To my surprise, I discovered that it had been released on Steam, and I couldn't resist giving it another go!\r\n\r\nThe closest game I can think of to compare it to is Happy Wheels. You play through roughly 30 levels, with the difficulty gradually increasing as you progress towards the game's conclusion. Along the way, you can customise your character, and there's something about the way the game presents him that makes you genuinely root for the little guy. Despite its relatively simple premise, it manages to be surprisingly charming.\r\n\r\nThe art style is absolutely brilliant and is definitely one of the main reasons I'm so fond of this game. It has a distinctive look that gives it so much personality, and it really helps Icycle stand out from other games in the genre. It's one of those games where the presentation is just as memorable as the gameplay itself.\r\n\r\nI'm also really glad that the game eventually made its way to PC. While the original mobile version was great, some of the later levels could be quite frustrating to complete on a tiny iPhone screen. Playing on PC makes these sections much more manageable, and it's nice to have a version that feels more comfortable to play through, especially when tackling the harder levels.\r\n\r\nAnother thing I really like is that every level has four achievements to work towards, giving you plenty of reasons to revisit stages you've already completed. There's a surprising amount of replayability here, particularly if you're someone who enjoys going back and trying to get everything done. I'm currently replaying the game myself with the goal of unlocking all the Steam achievements, which has given me another excuse to spend more time with it.\r\n\r\nI only have two minor complaints, neither of which should take away from the overall experience, but I think they're worth mentioning.\r\n\r\nFirstly, Icycle is still very much a mobile game at its core, and unfortunately, the PC port reflects that in a few ways. The game runs beautifully and looks perfectly fine on PC, but the settings menu is incredibly barebones, offering little more than a language selection. There's no option to adjust the screen resolution, change the controls or alter the GUI scale. None of these are major issues, and they certainly haven't stopped me from enjoying the game, but a few additional settings would have made the PC version feel much more complete.\r\n\r\nMy second complaint is even more minor. If you uninstall the game, your save data doesn't sync with Steam Cloud, meaning you could potentially lose your progress if you remove the game and reinstall it later. It's not a massive issue, especially considering how enjoyable the game is to replay, but it would be a welcome quality-of-life improvement. It's just something worth keeping in mind if you're planning to work towards all the achievements.\r\n\r\nOverall, though, I absolutely love Icycle. It's a charming, memorable indie game with a fantastic art style, increasingly challenging levels and plenty of replayability. There's something special about revisiting a game you remember from years ago and discovering that it holds up just as well as you remember. I'm genuinely glad I stumbled across the Steam version, and I can easily see myself returning to it again in the future.\r\n\r\n10/10. I would absolutely recommend giving this game a try, especially if you enjoy indie games with plenty of personality!",
